Emma — a preschooler-aged little girl with brown wavy ear-length hair pulled into a ponytail with a side part, brown eyes, soft rounded features, freckles, and small stud earrings, wearing her standard outfit — stands on the third step of a narrow wooden staircase inside the Geräteschuppen-Keller, body facing the camera, both hands gripping a small dark walnut wooden box with a brass latch as she lifts it from the low wooden shelf built against the rough pale grey stone wall at the bottom of the stairs directly below her. Noah — a kindergartner-aged little boy with blonde wavy ear-length hair with bangs at the eyebrows and blue eyes, soft rounded features, wearing his standard outfit — stands on the fourth step of the same staircase, one step above Emma and behind her in the midground, body facing Emma, one hand reaching toward her and the box as if ready to take hold. The staircase is narrow, running steeply from the lower left where the shelf sits up toward the upper right where an open door lets in a pale rectangle of warm light; that light band falls across the upper steps and across Noah's shoulders, leaving Emma and the shelf in dimness below. The rough stone walls press in on both sides of the staircase. Close-up shot, dim warm light from above, tense and determined mood.

